content.text:
Run both scripts on the project page, not inside a chat. On the project page the URL is `claude.ai/project/<uuid>`, so the project ID is read straight from the URL with no inference. Both scripts do fall back to reading the project ID from an open chat's payload, but that path depends on the conversation object exposing a `project_uuid` field, which is one of the guessed field names; the project page removes that dependency. Open the project, confirm the URL shows `/project/...`, then paste and run.

For the knowledge documents as a zip: the reason I steered away from a zip last time was that pulling in JSZip means injecting an external script, which Claude.ai's content-security policy may refuse. The way around that is to not use a library at all. A ZIP with stored (uncompressed) entries is a simple, fully specified binary format, and since your knowledge files are text the lack of compression costs nothing. The script below hand-builds the zip with a small CRC-32 routine and the standard local-header / central-directory / end-record layout, so it needs no external code and CSP cannot block it.

Run this discovery snippet first to confirm where the docs live and what field holds their content, since the docs endpoint is inferred (only the per-conversation endpoint was ever confirmed):

```javascript
// discovery — run on the project page, read the console
(async () => {
  const ck = n => (document.cookie.match(new RegExp('(?:^|;\\s*)' + n + '=([^;]+)')) || [])[1];
  const org = decodeURIComponent(ck('lastActiveOrg') || '');
  const pid = (location.pathname.match(/\/project\/([0-9a-f-]{36})/i) || [])[1];
  const get = async p => { const r = await fetch(p, { credentials: 'include' }); return { ok: r.ok, status: r.status, body: r.ok ? await r.json() : null }; };
  console.log('org:', org, 'project:', pid);
  const detail = await get(`/api/organizations/${org}/projects/${pid}`);
  console.log('project detail keys:', detail.body && Object.keys(detail.body));
  const docs = await get(`/api/organizations/${org}/projects/${pid}/docs`);
  console.log('/docs status:', docs.status, 'shape:',
    Array.isArray(docs.body) ? `array(${docs.body.length})` : docs.body && Object.keys(docs.body));
  const one = Array.isArray(docs.body) ? docs.body[0] : (docs.body && (docs.body.data || docs.body.documents || [])[0]);
  console.log('first doc item:', one);
})();
```

Look at `first doc item` and confirm it has a name field and a content/text field. If `/docs` returns 404, check whether `project detail keys` includes something like `docs`; the export script tries both.

